{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-93803",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$ Moreover, if $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n({x}_{0}+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,{y}_{0}-\\frac{a}{d}t),\\,t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=3,$ $b=7,$ $c=5,$ and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $(4,-1)$ is a solution of $3x+7y=5,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\frac{7}{1}t,\\,-1-\\frac{3}{1}t) & =(4+7t,\\,-1-3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
